{"id": "e1916e1b01b22c351d7a9e40547e31682502a3f5", "candidate_id": "martinez_rutherford", "candidate_name": "Dina Nina Martinez Rutherford", "collector": "wi_outlet_captimes", "title": "Opinion | Francesca Hong's making sure everyone eats on Thanksgiving", "text": "The Cap Times is an independent newsroom. Sign up for the Opinion Roundup newsletter for weekly Madison and Wisconsin viewpoints. Of all the strange twists of the 2026 election season, one of the most absurd was the determination on the part of conservative commentators and political prognosticators to make an \"issue\" of the eating habits and holiday approaches of popular progressives. Wisconsinites saw it when Francesca Hong was attacked by Republicans and some Democrats for her reflections on Thanksgiving. Hong was fiercely criticized for weeks, despite the fact that the chef-turned-gubernatorial candidate made it clear she loved celebrating holidays — especially the ones that focus on feeding people. But this wasn’t the only state where eating habits and holidays became campaign “issues.” President Trump and his allies went after Texas Senate candidate James Talarico with a false claim that the Democrat — who had once spoken about reducing meat consumption as part of broader efforts to address climate change — didn’t eat meat. \"He’s a vegan — a vegan in Texas. You can’t get elected as a vegan in Texas,” announced the president, while his conservative allies decried “Tofu Talarico.” Even after fact-checkers identified the presidential accusation as untrue and “unsupported by evidence,” and the candidate ate meat in public, Trump kept up the assault. He tried to get “Talafreako” going as a campaign-trail insult — only to be upended when Democrats gleefully donned T-shirts emblazoned with the phrase. Talarico eventually won by a 12-point margin, becoming the first Democrat in decades to hold a Texas U.S. Senate seat. Michigan Democrat Abdul El-Sayed — who in January will become the first Muslim member of the Senate — took even harder hits, as Trump attacked the American-born Rhodes Scholar as a \"jihadist\" who would “have to go through a metal detector before he’s allowed to go into the Senate.” Sen. Ted Cruz, R-Texas, labeled El-Sayed a “jihadist leprechaun.\" Cruz also claimed on Fox News that El-Sayed was part of a Democratic cabal that was “crazy enough” to eliminate popular holidays. “They want to cancel Thanksgiving. They want to cancel Christmas. Look, these communists hate America. They hate capitalism. El-Sayed is also an Islamist, as is (New York City Mayor Zohran) Mamdani. … They want Sharia law.” That attack came after conservatives unearthed a deleted tweet from Thanksgiving 2019 in which El-Sayed recognized the brutal treatment of Indigenous peoples — over the centuries after the first Thanksgiving — by British colonists and the U.S. government. That wasn’t exactly a shocking sentiment, as the Senate in 2009 approved the Native American Apology Resolution, which recognized a history of massacres, dislocations, broken treaties, forcible removals of Native children from their families and the theft of resources from recognized tribal land. The House added the resolution to the 2010 defense appropriations bill, and President Barack Obama signed it. Though it was limited in scope and shamefully underplayed at the time of its signing and afterward, that resolution apologized \"on behalf of the people of the United States to all Native peoples for the many instances of violence, maltreatment, and neglect inflicted on Native peoples by citizens of the United States.” Months before Cruz went after him, El-Sayed and Donavan McKinney, a Democrat who this month was elected to a Detroit area congressional seat, were busy delivering food to families in November 2025 and tweeting, “Everyone deserves Thanksgiving Turkey — even if Trump wants to deny them. That's why we took some time off the trail to hand out turkeys.” Thanksgiving became a bizarrely big “issue” in Wisconsin’s 2026 gubernatorial campaign, a detail that Hong recalled last week when she posted a picture of herself wearing a turkey costume to promote this year’s Thanksgiving basket food drive at Madison’s Goodman Community Center. In addition to urging Madisonians to drop off donations of canned cranberry sauce and brownie mix, the Democrat wrote, “If you come by Saturday with lots of donations you just might see someone who isn't cancelling Thanksgiving in a turkey costume.” Hong was referencing her Democratic primary campaign for governor, in which the grassroots candidate rose from relative obscurity to lead the polls. As the election approached, Hong suddenly found herself being portrayed in national media — and by some local outlets — as a Thanksgiving-bashing extremist who had no place seeking Wisconsin's top job. The media frenzy went on for weeks, as critics sought to turn the legislator into a caricature of herself by digging up old tweets and statements. Because Hong had, like El-Sayed, challenged Thanksgiving narratives that neglected to recognize harms done to Native peoples, she was mocked and ridiculed. The holiday \"issue\" was not the only factor in the late turn that the primary contest took, and Hong is quick to admit she didn't get everything right. But the attacks were relentless and, ultimately, she lost the primary by around 3,500 votes out of almost 800,000 cast. Hong immediately became an ardent campaigner for David Crowley, the Democratic nominee who on Nov. 3 won the governorship. For Madisonians who have known Francesca Hong over the years, the media meltdown over Thanksgiving seemed especially absurd. As a chef and longtime advocate for anti-hunger initiatives, she has for years stepped up as one of the hundreds of volunteers for the Goodman center’s Thanksgiving drive. If there's anyone who embraces the spirit of generosity and community that sums up what’s best about Thanksgiving, it's Francesca Hong. When she promoted this year’s Goodman food drive, and when she showed up for her volunteer shifts, Hong was just doing what she's always done: making sure that everyone can put food on the table in a moment where hunger is the real issue. And she's not stopping there. Hong has a well-chosen list of groups that are working to ensure that no one goes hungry, including the Goodman Community Center , Rooted , REAP and The Open Seat at UW-Madison. Give generously!", "source": "The Capital Times", "source_url": "https://captimes.com/opinion/john-nichols/opinion-francesca-hongs-making-sure-everyone-eats-on-thanksgiving/article_1ad5a287-b3e2-44ef-ae20-e30be13b0ed3.html", "resolved_url": "https://captimes.com/opinion/john-nichols/opinion-francesca-hongs-making-sure-everyone-eats-on-thanksgiving/article_1ad5a287-b3e2-44ef-ae20-e30be13b0ed3.html", "url_resolution": "direct", "text_source": "full_text", "full_text_status": "ok", "published_at": "2026-11-24T11:00:00+00:00", "reason": "no_name_match"}
